Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design time-bound cross-account access for external partners and maintain auditability across accounts.

Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- STS AssumeRole with session duration
- S3 Access Points and bucket policies per tenant
- KMS CMK rotation and envelope encryption
- CloudTrail multi-account logging and integrity
- Automated expiry and revocation mechanisms; breach simulations

Code Example

```javascript
// Example: using AWS SDK v3 to assume a role and upload to S3
import { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-sts";
import { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-s3";

async function uploadWithTemporaryCredentials(roleArn, bucket, key, body) {
  const sts = new STSClient({ region: "us-east-1" });
  const cred = await sts.send(new AssumeRoleCommand({ RoleArn: roleArn, RoleSessionName: "partnerUpload", DurationSeconds: 900 }));
  const s3 = new S3Client({ region: "us-east-1", credentials: cred.Credentials });
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key, Body: body }));
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and respond to a compromised tenant session expiry breach?
- What changes would you make to support a new partner with slightly different data retention requirements?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design automated, cross-account containment with tenant isolation, key lifecycle control, and immutable logs. Demonstrates threat modeling, policy boundaries, and incident runbook integration.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation across multiple AWS accounts
- Tenant-scoped KMS keys with rotation and key policy constraints
- Anomaly detection via CloudTrail Insights and GuardDuty
- Automated containment using EventBridge + Step Functions
- Immutable audit trails in CloudTrail and S3

## Code Example
```json
// Example KMS Key Policy (tenant-scoped)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Id": "tenant-key-policy",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/tenant-access"},
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt", "kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"aws:SourceAccount": "TENANT_ACCOUNT"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment workflow end-to-end before production?
- What metrics signal health of the containment and prevent false positives?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical containment thinking in a real multi-tenant AWS setup, focusing on automated detection, precise policy scoping, and rapid remediation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation with least privilege in API Gateway resource policies
- EventBridge-driven containment and CloudTrail immutability
- Cross-account trust scoping and rapid revoke of principals

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "execute-api:Invoke",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:execute-api:*:*:*/prod/*/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": { "aws:PrincipalAccount": "111111111111" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this containment flow in a CI/CD environment?
- What logging and auditing would you ensure are preserved regardless of containment steps?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical, beginner-friendly data erasure controls across accounts with auditable trails.

## Key Concepts

- S3 bucket versioning and Object Lock in Governance mode
- Cross-account Step Functions / IAM roles
- KMS CMK rotation and per-tenant keys
- CloudTrail Lake for immutable audit logs
- Access revocation via policy updates and least privilege

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: trigger erasure workflow
if (erasureRequested(tenantId)) {
  stepFunctions.startExecution({
    input: { tenantId }
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with minimal tenant impact?
- How do you handle backups and replication to ensure complete erasure?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-account trust, least privilege S3/KMS usage, no Internet constraint, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account role trust
- S3 bucket policy with path-level scoping
- KMS policy for decrypt/encrypt
- No Internet access
- Immutable auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy excerpt for DataAccessRole
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ProcessorRole"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```
```javascript
// Bucket policy excerpt (data-inbound)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-inbound",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-inbound/data-inbound/*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-out/*"
    }
  ]
}
```
```javascript
// KMS key policy snippet (CMK in Security account)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataAccessRole"},
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt","kms:GenerateDataKey*","kms:Encrypt"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for privilege creep?
- How would you rotate the CMK without downtime?
